  <!-- Adapter vs Converter -->
  <h2 id="adapter-vs-converter">Adapter vs Converter Explained</h2>
  <p>
    This confuses people. Here's the simple version:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Adapter</strong> changes the plug shape so it fits. That's it. Your US plug fits a European outlet.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Converter or transformer</strong> changes the voltage. Like 220V to 110V. Heavy, expensive, usually
    unnecessary for most of us.
  </p>

  <h3>Do You Need a Converter?</h3>
  <p>
    Check your charger. Actually pick it up and read the tiny text. If it says <strong>INPUT: 100-240V</strong>
    you're good with just an adapter.
  </p>
  <p>
    That includes basically everything modern:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Laptop chargers (all of them)</li>
    <li>Phone and tablet chargers</li>
    <li>Most camera chargers</li>
    <li>Electric shavers usually</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You might need a converter for hair tools mostly—dryers and curling irons often only handle US voltage. Some
    older electronics too. But honestly? For hair tools just buy dual-voltage versions or pick them up locally.
    Converters are heavy, can damage devices, and just aren't worth the hassle in my experience.
  </p>

  <!-- Plug Types by Region -->
  <h2 id="plug-types-region">Plug Types Around the World</h2>
  <p>
    Quick reference if you're wondering what you'll encounter:
  </p>
  <h3>The Americas</h3>
  <p>
    USA, Canada, and Mexico use Type A/B, those flat pins you know. Brazil had to be different with Type N.
    Argentina uses Type C and I.
  </p>

  <h3>Europe</h3>
  <p>
    Most places use Type C or F with two round pins. UK and Ireland use Type G, those three big rectangles.
    Switzerland has Type J. Italy uses Type L.
  </p>

  <h3>Asia and Middle East</h3>
  <p>
    China varies by city between Type A, C, and I. Japan uses A/B same as the US which makes life easy. India
    is complicated with C, D, and M depending on where. Thailand mixes A, B, and C. UAE and Saudi Arabia use
    the UK Type G.
  </p>

  <h3>Down Under and Africa</h3>
  <p>
    Australia and New Zealand use Type I. South Africa does Type M and N.
  </p>

  <!-- Safety -->
  <h2 id="safety">Safety Stuff That Actually Matters</h2>
  <p>
    Not all adapters are safe. Cheap knockoffs exist and they're sketchy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Look for <strong>CE marking</strong> which is the European standard, <strong>FCC certification</strong> from
    the US, <strong>UL listing</strong> which means real testing, and <strong>RoHS compliance</strong> for
    hazardous materials.
  </p>
  <p>
    Warning signs: No certifications listed anywhere on the package. Suspiciously cheap pricing. No brand name.
    No safety fuse. A bad adapter can fry your expensive laptop or worse, start a fire. Not worth saving ten bucks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything in this guide has proper certifications. I checked before including them.
  </p>

